🔐 Kubernetes ServiceAccount
Simple definition

ServiceAccount Kubernetes ke andar running applications/Pods ki identity hoti hai.

Abhi RBAC mein humne dekha:

User / Group / ServiceAccount
            ↓
        RoleBinding
            ↓
           Role
            ↓
       Permissions

Yahan ServiceAccount ek human user nahi hai. Ye generally Pod/application ki identity hoti hai.

🧠 Real-life example

Maan lo tumhara ek application Pod hai:

Backend Pod
    ↓
Kubernetes API ko request karni hai
    ↓
"kubectl get pods" jaisa API request

Kubernetes ko pata hona chahiye:

"Ye request kis identity ki taraf se aa rahi hai?"

Yahan ServiceAccount kaam aata hai.

Backend Pod
    ↓
ServiceAccount
    ↓
Kubernetes API Server
1️⃣ ServiceAccount create karna
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount

metadata:
  name: backend-sa
  namespace: default

Apply:

kubectl apply -f serviceaccount.yaml

Check:

kubectl get serviceaccount

ya:

kubectl get sa

Output:

NAME         SECRETS
backend-sa   ...
default      ...
2️⃣ Pod ko ServiceAccount dena

Ab tumhare paas:

backend-sa

Pod mein:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod

metadata:
  name: backend

spec:
  serviceAccountName: backend-sa

  containers:
    - name: backend
      image: nginx

Important line:

serviceAccountName: backend-sa

iska matlab:

Ye Pod backend-sa ServiceAccount ki identity use karega.

3️⃣ ServiceAccount + RBAC 🔥

Ab maan lo backend application ko Pods read karne ki permission chahiye.

Pehle Role:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role

metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: default

rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]

Ab RoleBinding:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding

metadata:
  name: backend-binding
  namespace: default

subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: backend-sa
    namespace: default

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

Ab complete flow:

                Backend Pod
                    ↓
              backend-sa
             ServiceAccount
                    ↓
              RoleBinding
                    ↓
              pod-reader
                    ↓
        get/list/watch Pods

Matlab:

Backend Pod backend-sa identity ke through Pods ko read kar sakta hai.

4️⃣ Ye actually useful kyun hai?

Suppose tumhara application Kubernetes API se information lena chahta hai.

For example, ek monitoring application:

Monitoring App
     ↓
Kubernetes API
     ↓
"Kitne Pods running hain?"

Agar application ko:

get pods
list pods

ki permission chahiye, toh ServiceAccount + RBAC use kar sakte ho.

Monitoring Pod
      ↓
monitoring-sa
      ↓
RoleBinding
      ↓
Role
      ↓
get/list Pods

Application ko cluster-admin dene ki zarurat nahi. ❌

Sirf required permission:

get
list
watch

de do. ✅

That's Least Privilege.

5️⃣ Default ServiceAccount

Kubernetes generally har namespace mein ek default ServiceAccount create karta hai:

default

Check:

kubectl get sa

Example:

NAME      SECRETS
default   ...

Agar Pod mein tum explicitly:

serviceAccountName:

nahi dete, toh Pod normally us namespace ke default ServiceAccount ko use karta hai.

So:

Pod
 ↓
serviceAccountName not specified
 ↓
default ServiceAccount
6️⃣ ServiceAccount vs User

Ye interview mein puch sakte hain:

User	ServiceAccount
Human identity	Application/workload identity
Developer/Admin etc.	Pod/Application
Human access ke liye	Workload access ke liye
Example: developer	Example: backend-sa

Simple:

Human
 ↓
User
 ↓
RBAC

Application
 ↓
ServiceAccount
 ↓
RBAC
7️⃣ ServiceAccount aur Secret ka relation

Purane Kubernetes versions mein ServiceAccount ke saath automatically long-lived token Secret create hona common tha.

Modern Kubernetes mein default behavior different hai: Pod ko API access ke liye generally short-lived, projected ServiceAccount tokens milte hain, rather than automatically creating a permanent token Secret.

Isliye blindly:

kubectl get secrets

mein ServiceAccount token Secret expect mat karna.

8️⃣ ServiceAccount + kubectl auth can-i

Permissions test kar sakte ho:

kubectl auth can-i get pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:default:backend-sa

Agar permission hai:

yes

Nahi hai:

no

Format:

system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<serviceaccount-name>

Example:

system:serviceaccount:default:backend-sa
🔥 Complete picture

Ab tumne RBAC + ServiceAccount dono padh liya, toh architecture aise yaad rakho:

                    RBAC
                     │
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          ↓                     ↓
        Human               Application
          ↓                     ↓
        User              ServiceAccount
          │                     │
          └──────────┬──────────┘
                     ↓
                  Binding
                     ↓
              Role / ClusterRole
                     ↓
                Permissions
                     ↓
        Kubernetes Resources
🧠 One-line interview answer:

A ServiceAccount is a Kubernetes identity used by Pods and applications to authenticate to the Kubernetes API and access resources according to permissions granted through RBAC.

Aur sabse important connection:

ServiceAccount = WHO (application ki identity)
Role = WHAT (kya kar sakta hai)
RoleBinding = WHO gets WHAT 🔥